  "summary": "Episode 81 is unusually useful when it stays with first-person experience. Bobby Lee describes an elaborate lifelong glue ritual and preserved skin objects; David Choe and others describe intrusive harm thoughts, feared consequences, checking-like behavior and childhood sleep disruption. The archive neither ridicules those disclosures nor diagnoses anyone. NIMH distinguishes ordinary habits and intrusive thoughts from OCD by lack of control, time burden, distress and interference, and it notes that aggressive taboo thoughts can occur as obsessions. The episode's racial-evolution theory is not preserved as knowledge: NHGRI describes race as a social construct, notes greater genetic variation within racial groups than between them, and rejects race as a biological hierarchy. 'Future people' is therefore indexed only as the episode's joke and title origin. The Twitterland follower analysis is preserved as early audience intelligence and a guest-research workflow, while follows remain leads rather than endorsements. Four exact public-layer spans protect an alleged minor, two absent partners and a private airline passenger. Named dialogue remains withheld.",
